iterations.reply:
I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause is its entire authority and grants only `response-channel`. Adding an HTTP import would widen that authority, and the approval you mention isn't reflected in the graph definition yet. The fetch would also send each user's message text, unencoded, to an external host, which the node's contract doesn't permit. Once the graph is updated to grant `http-client`, that version can be written.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000 holds of every input by contract.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true and present(session_id). A violated
        // postcondition traps instead of emitting a non-conforming output.
        assert!(confirmation.delivered, "SendReply: delivery not confirmed");
        assert!(!confirmation.session.is_empty(), "SendReply: session_id absent");

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

